WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.966
Have you ever been asked to do something that
felt a little scary — and said yes anyway?

00:00:04.966 --> 00:00:07.280
Maybe it was speaking in front of people.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.328
Maybe it was trying something you'd never
done before.

00:00:10.328 --> 00:00:13.488
Maybe it was just walking into a room full
of strangers.

00:00:13.488 --> 00:00:17.192
That feeling — that mix of nervous and
excited — has a name.

00:00:17.192 --> 00:00:26.636
Over the next few minutes, you're going to learn what it's called, why it
matters, and why the program you're starting today is built entirely around

00:00:26.636 --> 00:00:27.836
Welcome.

00:00:27.836 --> 00:00:29.784
You're about to begin something called
ACT.

00:00:29.784 --> 00:00:32.732
Let's start the way this program is meant
to start.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:34.987
بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ.

00:00:34.987 --> 00:00:36.432
Thank you for being here.

00:00:36.432 --> 00:00:41.955
I want you to know something right away: this
program is not going to feel like a classroom.

00:00:41.955 --> 00:00:43.155
It's casual.

00:00:43.155 --> 00:00:44.355
It's informal.

00:00:44.355 --> 00:00:46.517
You're allowed to be yourself in it.

00:00:46.517 --> 00:00:56.904
What you're starting is a series of exercises, activities, and games — and I want you to bring
real enthusiasm and initiative to all of it, because what you put in is what you'll get out.

00:00:56.904 --> 00:01:02.402
This program is called ACT — which stands
for Attitude Cultivates Transformation.

00:01:02.402 --> 00:01:11.904
That's exactly what it is: a program built on the idea that your
attitude — how you choose to face things — is what actually transforms

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:24.682
ACT is an interactive program designed to equip you with real skills for your future — the kind of skills the
job market actually asks for, and the kind that help you build your career — through social and voluntary work.

00:01:24.682 --> 00:01:26.301
It wasn't built generically.

00:01:26.301 --> 00:01:35.784
It was designed specifically with you in mind, and you've been chosen to be part of
it, alongside other young people who share the same bright future ahead of them.

00:01:35.784 --> 00:01:39.727
Before we go further, it's worth knowing
who's behind this.

00:01:39.727 --> 00:01:43.068
ACT is a global initiative by the NAMA
Foundation.

00:01:43.068 --> 00:01:49.550
Working alongside them is Ghadan — a not-for-
profit consultancy and capacity-building

00:01:49.550 --> 00:02:06.792
Take a second and think about that phrase, "not-for-profit." It means an organization that doesn't take any profit for its
partners or stakeholders — everything it does is aimed at its mission, which here is increasing social participation in your

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:18.155
There's also something ahead of you to look forward to: an awards ceremony, held across
Tanzania, where the strongest groups will be celebrated — with official guests in

00:02:18.155 --> 00:02:21.984
So everything you do in this program can
lead somewhere real.

00:02:21.984 --> 00:02:25.468
Here's the shape of the whole journey
you're starting.

00:02:25.468 --> 00:02:31.920
ACT is built from four units, and each one answers
a different question and builds different skills.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.550
Unit 1 is Self-Awareness.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.637
This is where you are right now.

00:02:35.637 --> 00:02:46.920
In this unit, you'll get to know yourself — your identity, your strengths, your
capabilities — through games and activities that are actually engaging, not just

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.651
Unit 2 is Social Awareness.

00:02:48.651 --> 00:02:58.136
Here, you'll become aware of your society — its strengths and its limits —
and understand what it really means to be part of a collective community.

00:02:58.136 --> 00:03:03.264
You'll watch examples from other societies
too, and keep learning through games.

00:03:03.264 --> 00:03:05.570
Unit 3 is Awareness of Social Issues.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:16.724
In this unit, you'll become aware of the social issues that actually exist around you,
understand how serious they are, and start thinking about how to contribute to solving

00:03:16.724 --> 00:03:23.952
You'll build a sense of social justice, and by the end,
you'll choose a social issue you personally want to work

00:03:23.952 --> 00:03:25.779
Unit 4 is Social Initiatives.

00:03:25.779 --> 00:03:32.772
This is the biggest unit — it covers half of the whole
program, and it's often the most fun and rewarding part.

00:03:32.772 --> 00:03:37.497
Here you'll learn the real fundamentals of
planning and managing a project.

00:03:37.497 --> 00:03:43.608
You'll choose your own project, build it, and
share what you achieved with the people around

00:03:43.608 --> 00:03:51.181
Notice the shape of that journey: who am I, who are others,
what do others struggle with, and what can I actually offer?

00:03:51.181 --> 00:03:55.536
That's the path ACT is going to walk you
through, one unit at a time.

00:03:55.536 --> 00:04:03.506
Before we go any further, let me tell you something about the person
guiding you through this program — and I want you to test yourself a

00:04:03.506 --> 00:04:06.145
I'm going to tell you three things about
myself.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:07.345
Two are true.

00:04:07.345 --> 00:04:08.545
One is not.

00:04:08.545 --> 00:04:09.168
See if you can guess which one.

00:04:09.168 --> 00:04:18.867
Here's something trainers in this program love to do on day one: they share two
true things about themselves, and one lie — and let the room guess which is which.

00:04:18.867 --> 00:04:24.674
It's a simple trick, but it works, because it
turns being taught at into getting to know

00:04:24.674 --> 00:04:30.362
So here's a challenge for you: think of two true
things about yourself, and one believable lie.

00:04:30.362 --> 00:04:33.416
Keep them in your head, or better, write
them down.

00:04:33.416 --> 00:04:37.367
You'll get a chance to try this out on
someone in your life today.

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:40.061
That's the spirit this whole program runs
on.

00:04:40.061 --> 00:04:42.456
So — how excited are you to get started?

00:04:42.456 --> 00:04:47.138
Let's begin the way this program always
begins: by breaking the ice.

00:04:47.138 --> 00:04:54.160
Grab a piece of paper — or your workbook if you have
one — and tear or cut it into three small pieces.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:05:00.013
On each piece, write one thing about
yourself: a hobby, a skill, or a personal

00:05:00.013 --> 00:05:01.321
Just one per piece.

00:05:01.321 --> 00:05:07.104
Take your time with this — it doesn't have
to be impressive, it just has to be true.

00:05:07.104 --> 00:05:08.304
Now it's your turn.

00:05:08.304 --> 00:05:09.585
Pause the video here.

00:05:09.585 --> 00:05:13.123
Write your three things — one hobby, one
skill, one trait.

00:05:13.123 --> 00:05:15.747
When you're done, come back and press
play.

00:05:15.747 --> 00:05:27.032
If you're watching together with a group: once everyone has their three papers, fold them,
mix them together, and have each person draw three at random, then go find who wrote each

00:05:27.032 --> 00:05:36.264
If you're watching alone, keep your three papers somewhere you'll see them
again — you'll be building on this idea of "who am I" throughout the program.

00:05:36.264 --> 00:05:40.019
That's a small moment, but it matters more
than it seems.

00:05:40.019 --> 00:05:44.631
You just put into words three real,
specific things about who you are.

00:05:44.631 --> 00:05:45.831
Hold onto that.

00:05:45.831 --> 00:05:52.536
Self-awareness — which is exactly what Unit 1 is
about — starts with small, honest moments like this

00:05:52.536 --> 00:05:58.691
Now that we've broken the ice, let's talk about
something important: the kind of space we want this to

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:06.761
Think about it for a second — what would you want from the people
around you, and from me, to make this program feel smooth and

00:06:06.761 --> 00:06:11.232
What would help you feel comfortable
enough to actually speak your mind here?

00:06:11.232 --> 00:06:24.231
Here are a few examples of the kinds of things that usually come up: putting phones away except for real
emergencies, showing up on time, and — maybe most importantly — nobody making fun of anyone else's ideas or

00:06:24.231 --> 00:06:30.072
Think about it this way: what do you think about
people interrupting each other while they're

00:06:30.072 --> 00:06:31.847
Should we agree not to do that?

00:06:31.847 --> 00:06:36.600
And what about people who laugh at someone
else's opinion while they're sharing it?

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:47.134
If you're watching this in a group: talk through these questions together, agree on your own
list of ground rules, write them down somewhere everyone can see, and have everyone sign it.

00:06:47.134 --> 00:06:54.024
If you're watching alone: pause here and write down, in your
own words, the two or three things you personally commit to.

00:06:54.024 --> 00:06:58.710
Here's the important part: this isn't a
list of rules being handed down to you.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:01.557
You are the ones deciding how this program
runs.

00:07:01.557 --> 00:07:05.472
That's what makes it a safe space — you
have real control over it.

00:07:05.472 --> 00:07:08.178
One more thing before we move on: a short
survey.

00:07:08.178 --> 00:07:18.120
There are no grades on this — it's simply a way to capture where your knowledge stands
right now, so that by the end of this journey, you can look back and see how far you've

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.320
Now it's your turn.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:25.392
Pause the video, and rate yourself honestly, right
now: I understand what ACT is and what it's for.

00:07:25.392 --> 00:07:28.091
I could name at least one of the four
units.

00:07:28.091 --> 00:07:30.483
I know what the 'challenge zone' means.

00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:33.549
I feel confident stepping outside my
comfort zone.

00:07:33.549 --> 00:07:35.635
I'm excited to start this program.

00:07:35.635 --> 00:07:40.725
Answer honestly, keep your answers
somewhere safe, and press play when you're

00:07:40.725 --> 00:07:44.064
We'll come back to this at the very end of
the program.

00:07:44.064 --> 00:07:45.264
Alright.

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:53.399
Now we get to the heart of this lesson — and honestly, to an
idea that's going to follow you through this entire program.

00:07:53.399 --> 00:07:54.878
Picture three circles.

00:07:54.878 --> 00:08:04.704
The first is your Comfort Zone — call it "Easy." The second is your Challenge
Zone — call it "Let's Go." And the third is your Pain Zone — call it "No Way!"

00:08:04.704 --> 00:08:06.830
Here's what each one actually means.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:13.327
The Easy zone is where you do things without needing to
think hard or put in any real effort — it's automatic.

00:08:13.327 --> 00:08:20.178
The Impossible zone is where you run into something you
genuinely don't know how to tackle, and it leaves you

00:08:20.178 --> 00:08:28.683
And the Let's Go zone — the challenge zone — is where you have some idea
how to tackle something, but not yet enough experience to do it easily.

00:08:28.683 --> 00:08:31.104
That's exactly what makes it a challenge.

00:08:31.104 --> 00:08:34.135
I'm going to walk you through five
situations.

00:08:34.135 --> 00:08:43.032
For each one, I want you to actually picture yourself in it, and
decide — honestly — which zone you'd be in: Easy, Let's Go, or No

00:08:43.032 --> 00:08:50.919
First: Suppose you're on a trip with your teacher and your
classmates, and suddenly the plane develops a technical problem mid-

00:08:50.919 --> 00:08:54.097
The pilot tells everyone to jump out with
a parachute.

00:08:54.097 --> 00:08:55.392
Which zone are you in?

00:08:55.392 --> 00:09:05.063
Second: You have to dive into the ocean, and you're told there are sharks nearby
— but the locals say these particular sharks are peaceful and won't hurt you.

00:09:05.063 --> 00:09:05.736
Which zone?

00:09:05.736 --> 00:09:10.311
Third: You have to stand up and speak in
front of 700 people.

00:09:10.311 --> 00:09:11.136
Which zone?

00:09:11.136 --> 00:09:17.475
Fourth: You're handed a strange meal, and
you have no idea what's actually in it.

00:09:17.475 --> 00:09:18.336
Which zone?

00:09:18.336 --> 00:09:26.386
Fifth: You have to voice your honest opinion in front of
important decision-makers — and you already know they disagree

00:09:26.386 --> 00:09:27.072
Which zone?

00:09:27.072 --> 00:09:33.837
Pause the video now, and for each of those five, decide
which zone you landed in — and if you can, think about

00:09:33.837 --> 00:09:44.544
If you're watching in a group: after each scenario, have everyone move to a different part
of the room representing their zone, and ask a few people to explain their choice out loud.

00:09:44.544 --> 00:09:46.929
Let's think through what just happened.

00:09:46.929 --> 00:09:50.781
What did you actually discover about
yourself in that exercise?

00:09:50.781 --> 00:09:54.939
Are the three zones clear to you now —
comfort, challenge, and pain?

00:09:54.939 --> 00:09:57.385
What's the real difference between them?

00:09:57.385 --> 00:09:59.587
Now think about how each zone feels.

00:09:59.587 --> 00:10:02.094
What do you feel toward the comfort zone?

00:10:02.094 --> 00:10:03.684
Toward the challenge zone?

00:10:03.684 --> 00:10:04.968
Toward the pain zone?

00:10:04.968 --> 00:10:06.579
Here's a harder question.

00:10:06.579 --> 00:10:11.541
Why did some of those situations feel
"easy" to you, and not to someone else?

00:10:11.541 --> 00:10:15.666
Why did some feel like "Let's Go," and
others like "impossible"?

00:10:15.666 --> 00:10:22.819
And think back on your own life — have the times you
felt genuinely challenged ever actually made you

00:10:22.819 --> 00:10:27.911
Does staying in the comfort zone ever
really grow your knowledge or your power?

00:10:27.911 --> 00:10:30.875
Does the pain zone add anything to you at
all?

00:10:30.875 --> 00:10:35.064
And does the challenge zone actually
benefit you, strengthen you?

00:10:35.064 --> 00:10:44.801
So here's the real question this whole exercise is pointing toward: which zone do
we actually need to spend our time in, if we want to keep growing and learning?

00:10:44.801 --> 00:10:51.998
A lot of people get this wrong at first — it's easy to
assume the answer is the comfort zone, because it's

00:10:51.998 --> 00:11:02.280
But the zone that actually grows your strengths and capabilities — the zone that
becomes the ladder toward becoming a capable, influential person — is the challenge

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:05.852
God created us with the ability to develop
and to advance.

00:11:05.852 --> 00:11:15.768
And the real difference between an exceptional human being and an average one
isn't talent — it's how much they've actually used and benefited from that

00:11:15.768 --> 00:11:25.992
The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: "Paradise is surrounded by
hardships." And hardship is about as close as it gets to the challenge

00:11:25.992 --> 00:11:28.883
Think about it — a person can grow so
much.

00:11:28.883 --> 00:11:39.910
Change for the better — psychologically, spiritually, emotionally, even financially
— just by taking one step: out of the comfort zone, and into the challenge zone.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:53.760
Youth — the stage of life you're in right now — is the very best period to learn, to face challenges,
and to grow in strength, knowledge, and power, for your own future and for the future of your community.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.882
This exercise, small as it seems, can
genuinely change your life.

00:11:57.882 --> 00:12:07.394
And here's something worth sitting with: many of us are serving our
community, in some real way, for the first time in our lives through this

00:12:07.394 --> 00:12:10.121
Children think enjoyment comes from
taking.

00:12:10.121 --> 00:12:17.160
But people who've actually lived know that the truest
pleasures in life are the moments of giving — not

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:28.728
This program — ACT — is built to deliberately put you in the challenge zone, again and
again, so that you walk out of it having genuinely added something new to who you are.

00:12:28.728 --> 00:12:32.046
Now let's see this in real life, not just
as a theory.

00:12:32.046 --> 00:12:43.596
It's the story of the Koh Panyee Island Soccer Team, from Thailand — a group of boys who built
their own soccer field out of nothing, in a place nobody expected a soccer team to come from.

00:12:43.596 --> 00:12:52.320
Pause the video now and watch the short film linked below — 'The Koh
Panyee Island Soccer Team.' When it's finished, come back and press

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:54.663
Take a moment with what you just watched.

00:12:54.663 --> 00:12:57.348
What did you actually see happen in that
video?

00:12:57.348 --> 00:13:00.776
How did people react when that soccer
field was first built?

00:13:00.776 --> 00:13:07.632
Why do you think their reactions changed once the team
actually started playing — and how did it feel watching them

00:13:07.632 --> 00:13:12.832
Why do you think those boys were able to beat
so many different, more established teams?

00:13:12.832 --> 00:13:16.968
And why were they able to keep playing
hard, even through bad weather?

00:13:16.968 --> 00:13:30.431
Here's the truth underneath that story: if it hadn't been for the harsh, uneven ground they had to play on — and the pain
they had to push through — they would never have developed the skill it took to beat those other teams under normal

00:13:30.431 --> 00:13:33.243
Hardship and challenge are what build
strong people.

00:13:33.243 --> 00:13:38.812
Whenever you're in the middle of a genuinely tough
situation, that's not a sign something's gone wrong.

00:13:38.812 --> 00:13:40.272
It's a sign you're growing.

00:13:40.272 --> 00:13:43.549
That same team went on to win seven
championships in a row.

00:13:43.549 --> 00:13:53.489
Facing a challenge can be hard at the beginning, and yes, you might even be mocked for it,
the way those boys were mocked for trying to play soccer on a wooden field on an island.

00:13:53.489 --> 00:14:02.208
But the same people who mocked them became their biggest supporters, the moment
they saw how skilled they'd become, and how much they believed in themselves.

00:14:02.208 --> 00:14:06.946
If you want to be a leader in your
community, hold onto that competitive

00:14:06.946 --> 00:14:11.508
Whenever a challenge comes your way, ask
yourself: what can I learn from this?

00:14:11.508 --> 00:14:14.901
What skill is going to come out of the
other side of this?

00:14:14.901 --> 00:14:24.552
Even everyday challenges — like having to work to earn money — are quietly teaching
you real, transferable skills that the job market values everywhere in the world.

00:14:24.552 --> 00:14:31.090
Today, you were welcomed into ACT — a program built on
the idea that your attitude is what transforms you.

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:42.623
